title The constant that answered like a function, and a price that was never about the kind of name
prompt Recur the autonomous loop -- pick up the running thread, close reds first, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, send each finished increment.
think Opened by reading KYRI and RADIANT_STYLE, then crux/REMEMBER.md, then the top three rows of session-logs/README.md. The newest log recommended asked_of as the fall head at 360.
think Standfast first. Living REDS rows 142, 143, 144 all read CLOSED, and 133-134 are archived. No open agent-closable red stood, so the line was clear for constructive work.
think The previous lap's read_the_reply lift stood finished in the working tree and uncommitted. Ran the Caravan choir, tame_style_check, width-check, and radiant_negation before committing it -- witness before narrative, even for a lap another turn authored. All GREEN at 108. Committed and pushed both remotes as 708683b679 before opening new work.
think Read the asked_of body by hand across all thirty-one rungs before consulting the meter. Six names climb outward: addressable, max_note_path_len, ask_path, Dir, settlement_of_byte, Settlement. Four already stood public in all thirty-one. Two stood private in all thirty-one -- ask_path as a function and Dir as a constant -- which is exactly the pair the meter priced at widens_fn=31 widens_const=31.
think Both answered. ask_path in each rung is already a three-line stub calling ladder_checks.ask_path, so the function column answered from underneath -- the harness holds the real body and the lifted body calls it directly rather than climbing back out. Dir is const Dir = std.Io.Dir, and caravan/ladder_checks.rye binds that same alias at its own head at line 66, so the constant column answered from above.
think Wrote the lifted body as pub fn asked_of(comptime rung: type, io: std.Io, address: []const u8) ?[2]rung.Settlement at the tail of caravan/ladder_checks.rye, reaching rung.addressable, rung.max_note_path_len, rung.settlement_of_byte and rung.Settlement through the type parameter, and calling this harness own ask_path and Dir directly. Each of the thirty-one rungs now holds a three-line stub.
obs Carry measured 61,920 -> 61,560 after the lift -- exactly 360, the number the scan formula returned before the lap began. Four laps running the arithmetic has named the fall before the meter read it, now across three families, two stub widths, and a column the arc had never priced.
obs The finding worth carrying is that a widening price is a fact about where a name stands in the rung rather than about the kind of name it is. asked_of was queued as a genuinely new column because a constant has neither of this arc's two answers available to it in principle. In practice both answers reached it -- the function from underneath and the constant from above -- because the constant was an alias for a standard type the harness already binds. The kind of the name predicted nothing; where the name stood predicted everything.
obs The narrower reading is that a private alias of a shared type is a false price. Thirty-one rungs each wrote const Dir = std.Io.Dir privately, and the meter honestly counted thirty-one openings, because it reads visibility off the rung. A reader who checks what the name aliases sees a cost that was never there. The meter stays right to price it -- it cannot know what a name means -- and the hand-read before the lift is what turned the price into zero.
obs answered_by repeats the same paired-column shape at thirty-four openings of each kind and is now pinned in the reach witness, so the next reader can check this lap's answer rather than trust it.
obs The council rota row read this lap was Fire and Mars -- cut and stop. Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. The row landed on the lap directly: the first act was to run every gate over an inherited working tree before adding a line to it, which is the stopped line applied to work the turn did not author. The three depths clarified that this arc is neither molt nor breach nor debride -- a lift moves a body and keeps every caller working, so accrete-never-break is undisturbed and no cairn is owed.
